{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>Is there a tree in it or not.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory (Bava Batra 15a) interpreted: if there is a worthy man there who can shield them like a tree that casts shade and protects all who shelter beneath it — and concerning this it was said, ״סָר צִלָּם מֵעֲלֵיהֶם״, for Iyov had already died. A hint to the matter: ״צִלָּם״ (their shade) has the same numerical value as ״עֵץ״ (tree). And some say that ״עֵץ״ here is עוּץ, which is Iyov, who came from the land of Utz. And according to the hidden meaning, he said ״הֲיֵשׁ בָּהּ עֵץ אִם אַיִן״ — it resembles what Israel said: ״הֲיֵשׁ ה׳ בְּקִרְבֵּנוּ אִם אָיִן״, for Israel were not in doubt about the existence of HaShem, blessed be He — for it was not said ״הֲיֵשׁ ה׳ אִם אָיִן״, but rather they knew that there is HaShem; rather, they were in doubt whether He was in their midst and watching over them, or whether He had delivered them under the governance of the celestial order. So too Mosheh wished to investigate whether that land dwells in the shade of Shaddai, a land upon which the eyes of HaShew continually look, or whether it is under the celestial order like all other lands. And he hinted, in the word ״עֵץ״, to the shade of Shaddai; and the Name of four letters is alluded to in the word עֵץ. How so: י״ה — י ten times ה, ה ten times י — behold one hundred. And ו״ה — ו five times ה, ה five times ו — behold sixty. The total sum is one hundred and sixty, equal to the numerical value of ״עֵץ״ and equal to the numerical value of ״צִלָּם״, and this is what it says: ״סָר צִלָּם מֵעֲלֵיהֶם״ — He abandoned them to chance encounters, וַה׳ אִתָּנוּ שׁוֹמְרֵינוּ וְצִלֵּנוּ. And from the fact that it says ״מֵעֲלֵיהֶם״, infer from this that His shade was upon that land for the sake of those other than them — the shade departed from the wickedness of its inhabitants. But the land in and of itself is a land upon which the eyes of HaShem continually look." "And he said: <b>And you shall strengthen yourselves and take of the fruit of the land.</b> Because the days were the season of the first-ripe grapes, and the owners are strict about taking; and from wherever they took, they strengthened themselves and acted as men, for HaShem is with you. And by way of allusion he said that Israel is likened to grapes, as it is written (Hosea 9:10): ״כַּעֲנָבִים בַּמִּדְבָּר מָצָאתִי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, and in the Midrash (Yalkut Shim'oni, Vayeshev, remez 140, see there) they said: ״הִבְשִׁילוֹ אֶשְׁכְּלוֹתֶיהָ עֲנָבִים״ — the time of Israel had arrived to be redeemed. Concerning this he said here, ״וְהַיָּמִים יְמֵי בִּכּוּרֵי עֲנָבִים״ — their time had arrived to enter the land, for they had already been first-ripened and ripened and the entirety of their perfection was completed through the Torah they had received."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {0 "Resolved abbreviation רז״ל as 'our Rabbis of blessed memory' and ילקו״ש as Yalkut Shim'oni; the closing phrase 'וה׳ אתנו שומרינו וצלינו' appears to be a liturgical refrain inserted into the argument rather than a direct biblical quotation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:41:08.386184879Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}